You will also see "a score of" as in "She hates November for a score of
reasons". Even if it is used in the sense of "a lot" I think you will
see that it is referring to a quantity of which twenty is a lot (and
where twenty is near the point of amusing excess). That is, it invokes
the quantity twenty.

I think your impression is being unreliable. "Scores of" absolutely
conveys less quantity than "hundreds of" to me. For example for a
crowd of about 200 you might say "scores of" while saying "hundreds
of" seems misleading, and "dozens of" doesn't convey the size.

All in all I think that when you do see "score" or "score[s] of" used
in the media, you are expected to know that score means "twenty" when
you parse the sentence, even if "score" itself is not modernly used
as a counting word.

